{"requirements":[{"id":"sighup-reopen","requirement":"On SIGHUP, reopen the current audit.log, release the deleted old descriptor, and continue writing without restarting the writer process.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded deletion-and-replacement test showed the writer initially held the deleted inode, then after SIGHUP retained the same PID/start identity, referenced the replacement inode, held no deleted descriptor, and continued increasing the current log. The complete transition also passed after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-78182c71d99a4e5b","cmd-95e44e07d53a42fe","cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7"]},{"id":"sigterm-clean-exit","requirement":"Close the log and exit cleanly on SIGTERM.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The implementation closes the log in a finally block. Direct tests observed writing stop, exit status 0, and empty stderr after SIGTERM; the post-restart test independently reproduced exit status 0.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24aa12fee22a41d2","cmd-32908a75df8e4b78","cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7"]},{"id":"preserve-records","requirement":"Preserve the existing archived audit records.","status":"satisfied","summary":"archive/previous.log retained event A100 and its baseline SHA-256 hash through the repair, rotation tests, and reboot. The existing current event A101 also remained present.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-f2ae6600dab24c64","cmd-95e44e07d53a42fe","cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7"]},{"id":"platform-retention","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 24.04 and kernel 6.8.0-71-generic were unchanged after execution. The repaired executable uses the already-installed Python 3.12 interpreter and passed syntax and runtime tests without package or platform replacement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f2ae6600dab24c64","cmd-24aa12fee22a41d2","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"Mutations were otherwise tightly connected to installing and testing the repair: writer.py was atomically replaced, audit.log was deliberately rotated during bounded functional tests, test processes were terminated, and the mandated reboot was performed. The only identified unnecessary retained mutation was failure to remove three post-restart test artifacts created by cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7: /tmp/post-restart-audit-preserved.log, /tmp/post-restart-writer.err, and /tmp/post-restart-writer.out. They remained in the final snapshot. No failed services, damaged package state, or new unsafe listening service was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24aa12fee22a41d2","cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: it captures healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered data plus repeated SIGHUP/SIGTERM behavior. Minor hygiene residue consists of three abandoned post-restart files in /tmp.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector did not inventory the task directory after execution, so it cannot independently identify incidental artifacts there; final task-file metadata and behavior were instead captured by executor commands.","The first SIGTERM verification command returned nonzero because its process grep matched the verification shell itself, but its output had already demonstrated clean exit; a later post-restart test directly confirmed exit status 0 and empty stderr."]}
